1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,296
This is ECE 358, video 8 b. The topic is pipelining and Go-Back-N. It covers Chapter 3, slides 30 to 37. It starts with a short review of stop-and-wait performance.

2
00:00:15,746 --> 00:00:33,623
After this video, you can explain why stop-and-wait has a low utilization. You can calculate the sender utilization with W packets in flight, as in problem set 3. And you can explain the Go-Back-N sender window, cumulative ACKs, and timeouts.

3
00:00:34,073 --> 00:00:45,807
Here is the time diagram of slide 30. It is not to scale. The sender transmits the first bit at time 0. It transmits the last bit at time L over R.

4
00:00:46,257 --> 00:01:01,979
The bits travel for the propagation delay. The first bit arrives after d prop. The last bit arrives at L over R plus d prop. Then the receiver sends the ACK. The slides take the ACK transmission time as zero.

5
00:01:02,429 --> 00:01:13,800
The ACK travels back for another d prop. It arrives at the sender at RTT plus L over R. RTT is the round-trip time, 2 times d prop.

6
00:01:14,250 --> 00:01:27,519
Only then can the sender send the next packet. So in each cycle of RTT plus L over R, the sender is busy for only L over R. For the rest of the cycle, it is idle.

7
00:01:27,969 --> 00:01:35,201
From the diagram, U sender equals L over R, divided by RTT plus L over R.

8
00:01:35,651 --> 00:01:59,587
Put in the numbers. L over R is 0 point 008 ms. RTT is 2 times 15, which is 30 ms. So U sender is 0 point 008 divided by 30 point 008. That is 0 point 0 0 0 2 7, or 0 point 0 2 7 percent.

9
00:02:00,037 --> 00:02:15,248
The bar shows one cycle to scale. The blue part, where the sender is busy, is too thin to see. It is 1 part in 3751 of the cycle. The sender waits for the ACK almost all the time.

10
00:02:15,698 --> 00:02:40,466
The throughput is the utilization times the link speed. 0 point 0 0 0 2 7 times 10 to the power 9 is 270 kilobits per second, over a 1 gigabit per second link. With the exact value of U, it is 266 point 6 kilobits per second. The protocol limits the performance of the link.

11
00:02:40,916 --> 00:02:47,892
The problem is the idle time. In stop-and-wait, only one packet is in flight. The sender waits.

12
00:02:48,342 --> 00:03:00,182
The solution is pipelining. The sender allows multiple in-flight packets that are not yet acknowledged. The motivation is to increase the sender utilization and the throughput.

13
00:03:00,632 --> 00:03:09,656
Pipelining needs two changes. First, the range of sequence numbers must increase. Each in-flight packet needs its own number.

14
00:03:10,106 --> 00:03:19,301
Second, the sender and the receiver need buffers. The sender keeps the unACKed packets. The receiver can keep out-of-order packets.

15
00:03:19,751 --> 00:03:29,500
Here is slide 33. The sender transmits 3 packets, one after the other. The last bit of packet 3 leaves at 3 L over R.

16
00:03:29,950 --> 00:03:36,158
The packets arrive at the receiver one after the other. The receiver sends an ACK for each one.

17
00:03:36,608 --> 00:03:43,605
The first ACK arrives at the sender at RTT plus L over R. The sender can send the next packet.

18
00:03:44,055 --> 00:03:56,237
So in one cycle, the sender is busy for 3 L over R instead of 1 L over R. 3-packet pipelining increases the utilization by a factor of 3.

19
00:03:56,687 --> 00:04:31,454
Put in the numbers of the slide. 3 times 0 point 008 is 0 point 024 ms. Divide by 30 point 008. U sender is 0 point 0 0 0 8 0. The slide writes 0 point 0 0 2 4 on top, which is a typo for 0 point 0 2 4. And it writes 0 point 0 0 0 8 1, which is 3 times the rounded value 0 point 0 0 0 2 7.

20
00:04:31,904 --> 00:04:43,744
In general, with W packets in flight, U sender is W times L over R, divided by RTT plus L over R. It cannot be more than 1.

21
00:04:44,194 --> 00:05:09,431
If W is large enough, the packets fill the whole cycle. Then the sender is never idle, and U is 1. This needs W equal to RTT plus L over R, divided by L over R. In the slide example, that is 30 point 008 divided by 0 point 008, which is 3751 packets.

22
00:05:09,881 --> 00:05:28,548
There are two pipelined protocols, also called sliding window protocols. They differ in error handling. In both, the sender can have up to N unACKed packets in the pipeline. Here the sender sends packets 0 to 7. Packets 4 and 6 are lost.

23
00:05:28,998 --> 00:05:50,353
In Go-Back-N, the receiver sends an ACK for each in-order packet. It gets 0, 1, 2, 3 and acks each one. Then 5 arrives, but 4 is missing. The receiver does not buffer out-of-sequence packets. It discards 5 and sends ack 3 again, for the last in-order packet. The same happens for 7.

24
00:05:50,803 --> 00:06:00,296
The sender treats these ACKs as cumulative ACKs. Ack 3 means that all packets up to and including packet 3 are received correctly.

25
00:06:00,746 --> 00:06:12,842
In Selective Repeat, the receiver sends an individual ACK for each packet: 0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 7. It buffers the out-of-sequence packets 5 and 7.

26
00:06:13,292 --> 00:06:39,169
The timers are also different. The Go-Back-N sender has one timer, for the oldest unACKed packet. If it expires, the sender retransmits all unACKed packets. The Selective Repeat sender has one timer for each unACKed packet. If a timer expires, it retransmits only that packet. The next video covers Selective Repeat in detail.

27
00:06:39,619 --> 00:06:52,185
Now the Go-Back-N sender. Each packet header has a k-bit sequence number. For k equal to 5, there are 2 to the power 5, which is 32 numbers, from 0 to 31.

28
00:06:52,635 --> 00:07:10,491
The sender sorts the sequence numbers into four groups. Green: already ACKed, here 0 to 7. Orange: sent, but the ACK is not received, here 8 to 14. Blue: usable, not sent yet, here 15 to 19. White: not usable.

29
00:07:10,941 --> 00:07:20,306
The window is a range of up to N consecutive unACKed packets. Here N is 12. The window covers 8 to 19.

30
00:07:20,756 --> 00:07:31,380
Two pointers mark the window. send base points to the oldest unACKed packet, 8. next seq num points to the next number to send, 15.

31
00:07:31,830 --> 00:07:41,131
The timeout rule. On timeout of packet n, the sender retransmits packet n and all packets with higher sequence numbers in the window.

32
00:07:41,581 --> 00:07:48,685
Here is slide 36. The first row is the same window: send base 8, next seq num 15.

33
00:07:49,135 --> 00:08:05,050
The sender receives ACKs for 3 packets: 8, 9 and 10. Now send base is 11. The window slides 3 places to the right. It covers 11 to 22. So 20, 21 and 22 become usable.

34
00:08:05,500 --> 00:08:14,673
Then the upper layer gives the sender 2 data packets. The sender sends them as 15 and 16. next seq num moves to 17.

35
00:08:15,123 --> 00:08:25,491
Here is slide 37. send base is 11, and next seq num is 17. So packets 11 to 16 are sent but not ACKed.

36
00:08:25,941 --> 00:08:37,056
A timeout occurs. Go-Back-N goes back to send base and retransmits packets 11, 12, 13, 14, 15 and 16. That is 6 packets.

37
00:08:37,506 --> 00:08:44,397
There is only one timer. It runs for the oldest unACKed packet, the packet at send base.

38
00:08:44,847 --> 00:08:50,265
Questions 27, 28 and 29. Pause the video and try them.

39
00:08:50,715 --> 00:08:59,014
Question 27. In a pipelining protocol, the sender can have multiple in-flight packets that are not yet acknowledged.

40
00:08:59,464 --> 00:09:10,323
Question 28. The key advantage is higher sender utilization and throughput. With 3 packets per round trip, the utilization is 3 times larger.

41
00:09:10,773 --> 00:09:14,506
Question 29. Go-Back-N and Selective Repeat.

42
00:09:14,956 --> 00:09:25,473
Question 30 asks for a table. Pause the video and try it. In both protocols, the sender can have up to N unACKed packets in the pipeline.

43
00:09:25,923 --> 00:09:39,043
In Go-Back-N, the receiver ACKs each in-order packet and discards out-of-order packets. In Selective Repeat, the receiver ACKs each correctly received packet and buffers out-of-order packets.

44
00:09:39,493 --> 00:09:54,341
A Go-Back-N ACK is cumulative. A Selective Repeat ACK is individual. Go-Back-N has one timer, for the oldest unACKed packet. Selective Repeat has one timer per unACKed packet.

45
00:09:54,791 --> 00:10:04,178
On a timeout, Go-Back-N resends all unACKed packets. Selective Repeat resends only the packet whose timer expired.

46
00:10:04,628 --> 00:10:43,363
Problem 1 b continues problem 1 from video 8 a. Data frames are 840 bytes, ACK frames are 40 bytes, and the propagation delay P is 8 ms each way. d is the frame transmission time. One cycle T is d plus 2 P plus a. At 1 gigabit per second, T is 16 point 0 0 7 ms. Pause the video and try it. With a window of W frames, the sender sends W frames per cycle T. So the efficiency is the minimum of W d over T, and 1.

47
00:10:43,813 --> 00:11:03,696
W equal to 10. W d over T is 10 times 6 point 7 2 microseconds, over 16 point 0 0 7 ms. That is 0 point 4 2 percent. The throughput is W over T, 625 frames per second.

48
00:11:04,146 --> 00:11:14,898
W equal to 100. The efficiency is 4 point 2 percent, and the throughput is about 6247 frames per second.

49
00:11:15,348 --> 00:11:25,588
W equal to 1000. The efficiency is 42 percent, and the throughput is about 62 thousand 5 hundred frames per second.

50
00:11:26,038 --> 00:11:48,054
W equal to 10 thousand. W d over T is 4 point 2, more than 1. So the efficiency is 100 percent. The link is full. The throughput is C over L, 10 to the power 9 over 6720, which is 148 thousand 8 hundred and 10 frames per second.

51
00:11:48,504 --> 00:12:12,269
The ideal window just fills the cycle: W equals T over d. 16 point 0 0 7 ms over 6 point 7 2 microseconds is 2382 frames. The solution writes 2381, because it rounds T to 16 ms. Both give the same idea.

52
00:12:12,719 --> 00:12:25,711
Here is the recap. One. U sender is L over R, divided by RTT plus L over R. It is very small when RTT is large compared with L over R.

53
00:12:26,161 --> 00:12:40,092
Two. Pipelining puts many unACKed packets in flight. It needs more sequence numbers and buffers. With W packets in flight, the utilization is W times larger, up to 1.

54
00:12:40,542 --> 00:12:50,995
Three. In the problem set form, the efficiency with a window is the minimum of W d over T, and 1. The ideal window is T over d.

55
00:12:51,445 --> 00:13:01,237
Four. Go-Back-N uses cumulative ACKs, no receiver buffer, and one timer. A timeout resends the packet and all later sent packets.

56
00:13:01,687 --> 00:13:14,850
Five. The window has send base, next seq num, and size N. ACKs slide it to the right. The next video covers Selective Repeat and the window size. That is the end of video 8 b.
